(i) Potassium reacts violently, giving out so much heat that the hydrogen formed catches fire: 2K + 2H_{2}O → 2KOH + H_{2} + heat
(ii) Calcium reacts less violently; bubbles of gas form on it: Ca + 2H_{2}O → Ca(OH)_{2} + H_{2}
Calcium floats because bubbles of hydrogen gas stick to its surface.

(i) Mg + 2H_{2}O → Mg(OH)_{2} + H_{2}
(ii) 2Al + 3H_{2}O → Al_{2}O_{3} + 3H_{2}
Copper (or silver, gold, lead) does not react even with steam.

X is sulphur and the oxide is sulphur dioxide.
S + O_{2} → SO_{2}
SO_{2} + H_{2}O → H_{2}SO_{3} (sulphurous acid, which is why litmus turns red)
